8. Business Categories and Unified Modules

The system must support different types of businesses through one shared software architecture.

Examples:

- Retail and wholesale shops.
- Dealer and dealership businesses.
- Restaurants and food businesses.
- Hotels and residential accommodation.
- Vehicle sales and rental businesses.
- Distribution and trading businesses.
- Manufacturing and production businesses.
- Other product-based commercial enterprises.

During company onboarding, let the Super Admin choose the business category and configure the relevant modules.

The core system must remain consistent across all business categories. Business-specific modules may be enabled when needed.

Small businesses

Provide a simple interface for a proprietor who manages the business personally.

Prioritize sales, purchases, stock, customer dues, supplier payables, daily cash transactions, expenses and profit summaries.

Medium businesses

Support two or three business units, branches or operational business areas according to the subscription plan.

Include employee accounts, role-based permissions, inventory management, dealer pricing, customer and supplier ledgers, business-unit reports and consolidated summaries.

Large businesses

Support five or six business units or more when the subscription plan permits.

Include advanced employee permissions, multiple branches, restaurant operations, hotel accommodation, vehicle-related operations, dealerships, distribution, manufacturing, warehouse management and consolidated financial reporting.

Do not hard-code a small number of branches or business units into the database schema.

All company and business-unit limits must be configurable by the Super Admin.

